### Custom Handling
Syntax
try {
    // risky code
} catch (ExceptionType e) {
    // custom handling logic
}
Key Points to Remember
. Specific First, Generic Last: Always list your specific exception handlers ( PaymentFailedException,
DriverlssueException , etc.) before the generic catch (Exception e) .
. One Match Only: Java stops at the first matching catch block-so order matters.
. Clear Messages: Each catch block should print a helpful message so you know exactly what went wrong.
. Safety Net: The generic catch is your final backup for any unexpected errors.
. Avoid Unreachable Code: Placing the generic catch too early makes specific handlers unreachable-and causes a
compile error.
Common Interview Questions & Answers

Q1: Why must a generic catch block come last?
A: Because catch (Exception e) matches all exceptions. If it appears first, it will catch every error, and the specific
handlers below it will never run.

Q2: What error do you get if you place a generic catch before a specific one?
A: A compile-time error: Unreachable catch block for [SpecificException]. It is already handled by the catch block for
Exception.

Q3: Can a generic catch block ever replace all specific catches?
A: Technically yes, but it's not best practice. You lose the ability to give tailored handling and clear feedback for different
error types.

